Generated Media Frontier =E2=80=94 1 material find

OpenShorts =E2=80=94 TEST NOW / MINE NOW
GitHub: https://github.com/mutonby/openshorts (current indexed forks expose
the same rapidly evolving codebase)

Meaningful delta verified in the current repository surface: OpenShorts has
evolved beyond a generic shorts generator into an agent-addressable
editing/production substrate. The current tree contains an MCP server plus
agent skill, Remotion renderer, scene detection, hook grounding, layout
selection, split-screen and screencast layouts, active-speaker logic,
recutting, punch-in, quality probing, aesthetic verification, thumbnail
generation, translation, and YouTube clients. The repository reports 420
commits. Self-hosting is MIT, Docker-based, and designed for 8GB+ RAM; the
README reports 5=E2=80=938 minutes CPU processing for an 8-minute source vi=
deo
versus ~50 seconds on its hosted NVIDIA path.

What it does: long video/local upload -> transcript + scene boundaries ->
AI-selected moments -> adaptive 9:16 layouts ->
captions/dubbing/hooks/effects -> finished clips; it also exposes an AI
Shorts/UGC path and MCP/API surface for agents.

Why technically interesting: this is increasingly the exact deterministic
post-generation/editing layer we need after expensive avatar generation. In
particular, its layout_picker, hook_grounding, quality_probe,
verify_aesthetic, recut, reframe_v2 and MCP surfaces mean an agent can
manipulate an existing expensive performance rather than regenerate video.
That can collapse marginal experimentation cost.

Inputs -> outputs: source video or generated actor assets + agent
instructions -> multiple social-ready 9:16
edits/layouts/hooks/captions/dubs.
Code: available. License: MIT for self-hosted code. Maturity: RUNNABLE /
approaching PRODUCTIONISH. Hardware: CPU-capable; README asks for 8GB+ RAM;
GPU materially accelerates processing.

Exact experiment: render ONE 20=E2=80=9330 second premium synthetic-host
performance, then use OpenShorts through MCP to generate 20 variants
varying first-frame hook, crop/layout, captions, punch-ins, B-roll
placement and CTA without rerendering the avatar. Feed retention
predictions/critic scores back into selection. Compare marginal cost and
quality against HyperFrames and our direct Remotion/FFmpeg path.

Replace/complement: complements HyperFrames. HyperFrames remains stronger
for arbitrary deterministic HTML motion graphics; OpenShorts is stronger as
an opinionated agentic social-video editor/reframer. Do not replace
Performance IR or the avatar renderer.

TOP 5 TO TEST RIGHT NOW

   1. OpenShorts MCP -> 20 edits from one premium avatar performance.
   2. HyperFrames -> same assets, same 20-edit benchmark.
   3. Performance IR -> replay identical acting through cheap VRM and
   premium renderer.
   4. NanoAvatar -> edge/live renderer benchmark.
   5. Avatar-Forever -> long-horizon identity/drift benchmark when suitable
   GPU is available.

BEST OPEN-SOURCE AI INFLUENCER STACK THIS HOUR
Persona/memory + audience history -> creative LLM -> Jev-like cheap
decision policy -> Performance IR -> cheap VRM/edge draft -> multimodal
critic -> premium avatar/video generation -> OpenShorts or HyperFrames
deterministic variation/editing -> publish -> platform analytics -> policy
update.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY JUMP
The post-generation layer is becoming agent-addressable. Expensive video
increasingly becomes a reusable source asset rather than the final artifact=
.

CHEAPEST PRACTICAL WIN
Generate one good avatar performance and derive many social edits from it
with deterministic/CPU-friendly tooling instead of paying for 20 video
generations.

WATCH NEXT

   - mutonby/openshorts and active forks
   - HeyGen HyperFrames
   - mobile/edge avatar projects around NanoAvatar

STACK DELTA
Add an EDIT IR between generated assets and platform output: hook text,
source ranges, crop/layout, caption style/timing, punch-ins, B-roll slots,
CTA, music and transition instructions. Keep this deterministic and
replayable. Performance IR controls the synthetic human; Edit IR controls
how that performance is packaged. This gives clean A/B experiments while
keeping expensive generation fixed.

Suppressed repeats: talk2avatar, Anima, MuseTalk wrappers,
threejs-talking-avatar and previously reported avatar systems had no
verified material delta worth repeating in this run.

